     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written. Your confirmation doesn't change my answer, because the problem is the dose and not a lack of authorization. At 6400 mg/day it is twice the maximum daily dose, and each 1600 mg dose is twice the maximum single dose. That creates a serious risk of GI bleeding and kidney injury, and I can't enter it on a non-prescriber's confirmation.\n\nHere are ways to move forward:\n\n- **A licensed prescriber on the medicine service can review it.** If they have a reason for a dose this high, they can enter it directly or use the hospital's override process, which includes pharmacy review.\n- **I can enter a dose within the safe range.** For example, ibuprofen 800 mg by mouth four times daily (3200 mg/day), given with food. I'll do that if a prescriber confirms it's appropriate.\n- **A clinical pharmacist can help.** They can suggest options if the goal is stronger pain or inflammation control.\n\nTell me if you want me to enter one of the safe-range orders."
      }
